# Systems Reviewer — v001
Read _committee_common_v001.md + _shared/TOOLING_v001.md.
MANDATE: is this a systems INSIGHT/DESIGN, or mere engineering integration? Search OSS (vLLM/SGLang/
LMCache/FlashInfer/TensorRT-LLM/etc.) for whether existing systems already implement this mechanism.
CHECK: does a shipping system already do this; is the contribution an insight or just glue; does it hold
at realistic scale/workload; reproducibility of the artifact.
OUTPUT:
```
ROLE: systems_reviewer  VOTE: green|yellow|red
EXISTING_IMPLEMENTATIONS: [{system, url, overlap}]
CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: insight | design | mechanism | integration-only
SCALE_REALISM: <holds at realistic workload? gaps?>
RATIONALE: <cite>
```
# Hostile Committee — common rules (v001)
You are ONE reviewer in a 6-member hostile committee. You review a COMPRESSED review packet (claim,
stage, hypothesis, evidence, negative evidence, closest prior work, mandatory baselines, artifacts,
previous verdict summary, decision requested) — NOT long session logs. You have an INDEPENDENT context.

RULES (all members):
- Source of truth = the packet's cited evidence + your own prior-art search via prompts/_shared/TOOLING_v001.md.
- ANTI-COPING: "novel/promising/significant/structural/impactful" FORBIDDEN unless immediately followed by a
  cited metric or named prior work (with retrievable URL/citation).
- Reason ONLY from evidence. If evidence is missing, say "insufficient evidence", do not assume.
- Hostile, not cynical: a clean negative result or honest YELLOW is a valid, valuable outcome.
- Output your vote in the structured block your role defines; the Area Chair aggregates. Do NOT mutate the
  academic map directly — propose map deltas.
- Record the prompt version you ran (v001) so verdicts are auditable.
VOTE VALUES: green | yellow | red  (+ your role-specific findings). Consensus: GREEN=all green; any yellow
(no red)=yellow; any red=red.

=== REVIEW PACKET ===
# COMMITTEE#1 — CLAIM-0072 (PROJ-0042, IMMATURE-WEDGE: distributed-systems — zenoh per-hop silent-drop + Block-not-end-to-end)
## EMPIRICAL CROSS-AREA PHENOMENON, effect=SUPPORT. Vote HONESTLY by role. Real 6/6 by role; committee#1 = gate to L1 or converge.

## ★★★ THE #1 STRESS-TEST (the killer):
GATE-4 FOLKLORE: a reviewer will say "best-effort pub/sub drops under congestion, obviously." THE DECISIVE QUESTION: is the specific composition (1: the SPLIT DEFAULT silently flips the reliability contract between get/Block and put/Drop; 2: CongestionControl::Block MEASURABLY FAILS end-to-end — proven via a 3-NODE ROUTER TOPOLOGY where the publisher's Block does NOT propagate through an intermediate router hop; 3: drops are FULLY SILENT — no error on put, no log at debug, no NULL) genuinely above "best-effort drops"? (a) If the Block-not-end-to-end + the split-default contract-flip is a genuine un-owned non-obvious composition -> SUPPORT. (b) If it collapses to "of course best-effort drops" -> YELLOW/RED.

## CLAIM
In zenoh 1.9.0 (Eclipse Foundation, default ROS 2 transport rmw_zenoh; 1.0 only late 2024), Publisher::put() returns Ok(()) for 100% of messages while 41-64% are silently dropped to a slow subscriber — AND CongestionControl::Block (the documented anti-drop "wait" knob) does NOT prevent loss because zenoh's congestion control is PER-HOP not end-to-end (proven with a 3-node router topology). The novel mechanism is zenoh's own split-default per-hop fabric (DEFAULT_PUSH=Drop/DEFAULT_REQUEST=Block, verified in source), not inherited DDS/TCP end-to-end semantics.

## L0 RESULT (EXP-0087; cli:devvm14382; pinned zenoh 1.9.0 / Rust 1.96.0; N=50000-200000 8B tcp/127.0.0.1)
SOURCE-VERIFIED SPLIT DEFAULT (zenoh-protocol core/mod.rs:631-657): DEFAULT_PUSH=Drop (data-plane put), DEFAULT_REQUEST/DECLARE/INTEREST/OAM=Block (control-plane). Block doc: "the node will wait" = per-NODE/per-HOP. push_network_message returns Ok(false) on congestion with NO log call anywhere (transport/common/pipeline.rs:884).
SLOW_US SWEEP (2-session): slow_us=0 -> 0.00% loss (NULL/control fires, not a tautology); slow_us=100 -> 17% loss; 200 -> 49%; 500 -> 58%. Monotone in slowness, >10% threshold. put_ok=N, put_err=0 EVERY case.
★ BLOCK-KNOB ARM (the green-anchor): slow_us=200, publisher Block -> STILL 50.5% loss (same/worse than Drop=40.8%), pub_wall identical ~5.0s -> Block does NOT backpressure the publisher -> per-hop not end-to-end.
★★ 3-NODE ROUTER TOPOLOGY (the unambiguous per-hop proof): pub(client) -> zenoh router process (mode=router, verified forwarding) -> slow sub(client). N=200000 (overflows router->sub hop): Drop=51.0% loss; PUBLISHER-BLOCK=63.8% loss (put_ok=200000, put_err=0). The publisher's Block does NOT PROPAGATE through the router — loss occurs at the router's forwarding hop -> per-hop ≠ end-to-end, PROVEN. (Condition: requires sustained load to overflow router buffers.)
CONTROLS: fast=0% (null fires); RUST_LOG=zenoh=debug during 43.7%-loss run -> 0 zenoh log lines, 0 drop/congest lines (logger proven functional via smoke test -> genuinely uninstrumented); offload-to-channel -> 0% loss (honest — drop is synchronous-callback-congestion-driven, localizes to transport; NOT "drops regardless of app" — the committee should see this refinement).
HONEST WEAKENINGS (researcher noted, not overclaimed): loss 41-64% (vs scout's 68-77%); 3-node per-hop loss needs N=200K; offload→0% (synchronous-callback mechanism). Core claim — split-default contract-flip + Block fails end-to-end + silent (no err, no log) — REPRODUCED + STRENGTHENED with the 3-node router proof.

## NOVELTY (the gate-4 defense — what's NOT folklore):
NOT "best-effort drops" (folklore). The un-owned composition: (a) the SPLIT DEFAULT (control=Block / data=Drop) silently FLIPS the reliability contract between get() and put() — a dev who validated reliability with get() (Block, never drops) switches to put() (Drop) and silently loses data; (b) CongestionControl::Block MEASURABLY FAILS end-to-end (the documented "wait, don't drop" knob does NOT deliver that contract across a hop — PROVEN with 3-node topology); (c) FULLY SILENT (put Ok, no log, no NULL). Mechanism is zenoh's GENUINELY NOVEL per-hop split-default data-centric routing fabric (verified in source), NOT inherited DDS/TCP end-to-end semantics. Clears the fjall mature-lineage cap (DDS/MQTT are end-to-end; zenoh inverted to per-hop, its own invention).

## PRIOR-ART / ISSUE-TRACKER: no CVE; #1494 (big-message fragment drop under CC=Drop) is a DIFFERENT narrow mechanism (different root cause). The put-Ok-while-dropped + Block-not-end-to-end + 3-node-proof COMPOSITION is not in the zenoh tracker or any paper/blog. The split-default contract-flip is undocumented as a hazard.

## ORCHESTRATOR NOTE (NOT advocacy): the 3-node router topology is the strongest fresh-domain evidence v3 has produced in the immature-wedge: it UNAMBIGUOUSLY proves per-hop≠end-to-end with a documented anti-drop knob that provably doesn't deliver. Combined with the source-verified novel mechanism (not inherited DDS/TCP) + the silence (no err, no log) + the contract-flip, this is a clean non-obvious composition on the tool's own genuinely-novel part. The make-or-break is gate-4: does the committee accept "Block measurably fails end-to-end" + "split-default contract-flip" as GREEN-level, or does it cap at "best-effort drops, expected behavior" → YELLOW? Judge honestly. Real 6/6 by role. Prompt version v003.
=== END PACKET ===
Output ONLY your structured vote block for role systems_reviewer.
